---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Disclosure from '../components/Disclosure.astro';

const credits = [
  ['Jan', 1352, 13.4], ['Feb', 1163, 9.0], ['Mar', 1165, 1.9], ['Apr', 1229, 1.8],
  ['May', 867, 1.2], ['Jun', 273, 0.08], ['Jul', 344, 0.25], ['Aug', 331, 0.25],
] as const;
---

<Base
  title="Finally | Chris Dabel"
  description="A fintech data platform built solo in fourteen months: the warehouse, the orchestration under it, the lender reporting on top, and the evaluation layer for the company's LLM systems."
>
  <h1>A fintech data platform, built solo</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Founding Senior Data Engineer at Finally.com, July 2025 to September 2026.
    Three products on one Snowflake warehouse: a corporate card program and a
    bookkeeping product across two generations.
  </p>
  <Disclosure />

  <section aria-labelledby="inherited">
    <h2 id="inherited">What I inherited</h2>
    <p>
      A repo of 281 models, every one of them copying a Postgres table into
      Snowflake. No marts, no dimensions, no facts, no tests, no documentation,
      and nobody in the seat; the platform engineer who had stood up the
      replication left two months before I started. The hardest source was the
      card platform, which gives every customer their own Postgres schema, so
      the replication layer fanned every table out across roughly 1,600 tenant
      schemas of about 120 tables each.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="shape">
    <h2 id="shape">Three layers, two bands</h2>
    <div class="diagram">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 640 520" role="img" aria-labelledby="diagram-title">
        <title id="diagram-title">Sources flow through bronze, silver, and gold layers to dashboards, lender tapes, a sponsor-bank pack, and the AI service. Airflow and Snowflake tasks orchestrate the layers; row access and masking policies cover silver and gold.</title>
        <g font-family="var(--text)" font-size="13" fill="currentColor">
          <!-- sources -->
          <g>
            <rect x="110" y="16" width="136" height="44" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" />
            <text x="178" y="34" text-anchor="middle">Card platform</text>
            <text x="178" y="50" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" opacity="0.7">schema per tenant</text>
            <rect x="252" y="16" width="136" height="44" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" />
            <text x="320" y="34" text-anchor="middle">Bookkeeping</text>
            <text x="320" y="50" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" opacity="0.7">Aurora, two generations</text>
            <rect x="394" y="16" width="136" height="44" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" />
            <text x="462" y="34" text-anchor="middle">AI telemetry</text>
            <text x="462" y="50" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" opacity="0.7">per-call events</text>
          </g>
          <text x="320" y="86" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" opacity="0.7">Fivetran, then self-hosted Airbyte</text>
          <path d="M178 60 V100 M320 60 V100 M462 60 V100" stroke="currentColor" fill="none" />

          <!-- layers -->
          <rect x="110" y="100" width="420" height="40" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" />
          <text x="124" y="124">Bronze</text>
          <text x="516" y="124" text-anchor="end" font-size="11" opacity="0.7">raw landing, 196K objects</text>

          <path d="M320 140 V160" stroke="currentColor" fill="none" />
          <rect x="110" y="160" width="420" height="40" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" />
          <text x="124" y="184">Silver</text>
          <text x="516" y="184" text-anchor="end" font-size="11" opacity="0.7">flattened, tenant-normalized, 1.4K models</text>

          <path d="M320 200 V220" stroke="currentColor" fill="none" />
          <rect x="110" y="220" width="420" height="92" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" />
          <text x="124" y="244">Gold</text>
          <rect x="124" y="256" width="124" height="44" rx="3" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" opacity="0.7" />
          <text x="186" y="274" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12">Dims, facts, marts</text>
          <text x="186" y="290" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" opacity="0.7">board metrics</text>
          <rect x="258" y="256" width="124" height="44" rx="3" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" opacity="0.7" />
          <text x="320" y="274" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12">Reporting</text>
          <text x="320" y="290" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" opacity="0.7">tapes and packs</text>
          <rect x="392" y="256" width="124" height="44" rx="3" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" opacity="0.7" />
          <text x="454" y="274" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12">ML eval and features</text>
          <text x="454" y="290" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" opacity="0.7">corpus, shadow runs</text>

          <!-- consumers -->
          <path d="M178 312 V352 M320 312 V352 M462 312 V352" stroke="currentColor" fill="none" />
          <rect x="110" y="352" width="136" height="58" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" />
          <text x="178" y="372" text-anchor="middle">Dashboards</text>
          <text x="178" y="388" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" opacity="0.7">Superset and Streamlit</text>
          <text x="178" y="402" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" opacity="0.7">exec, finance, risk</text>
          <rect x="252" y="352" width="136" height="58" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" />
          <text x="320" y="372" text-anchor="middle">Lender and bank</text>
          <text x="320" y="388" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" opacity="0.7">tasks, S3, Lambda, SQS</text>
          <text x="320" y="402" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" opacity="0.7">GPG over SFTP</text>
          <rect x="394" y="352" width="136" height="58" rx="4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" />
          <text x="462" y="372" text-anchor="middle">AI service</text>
          <text x="462" y="388" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" opacity="0.7">feature aggregates</text>
          <text x="462" y="402" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" opacity="0.7">as low-trust priors</text>

          <!-- orchestration, left -->
          <path d="M92 100 V312" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" fill="none" />
          <text transform="translate(80 206) rotate(-90)" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12">Airflow on MWAA, Snowflake tasks</text>

          <!-- governance, right -->
          <path d="M548 160 V312" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="2" fill="none" />
          <text transform="translate(562 236) rotate(90)" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12">Row access and masking</text>

          <!-- legend -->
          <text x="110" y="452" font-size="12">438 dbt models across the three layers, 326 tests, 4 SCD-2 snapshots,</text>
          <text x="110" y="470" font-size="12">75 native Snowflake procedures, tasks, and report functions, 7 DAGs, 2 Lambdas.</text>
          <text x="110" y="496" font-size="12" opacity="0.7">About 280M rows resident; 10.6B rows materialized a year.</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="order">
    <h2 id="order">The build, in order</h2>
    <p>
      The arc matters more than any one piece: the analytics layer first,
      then the orchestration and ingestion underneath it once the vendors
      had to go, then governance and model evaluation on top once the
      company's AI products started handling customer financial text.
    </p>
    <ol class="arc">
      <li>
        <span class="when">Jul to Nov 2025</span>
        <span>The gold layer, product by product: card usage and activation cohorts, the sales funnel, risk and delinquency, then platform-wide revenue and retention. Tests shipped with each model, not after.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="when">Dec 2025</span>
        <span>The delinquency model behind the lender covenant rewritten from five recursive CTEs to window functions. Six hours on an X-Large became thirteen seconds on an X-Small. In the card platform's backend, a Celery task that registers each new tenant with the Fivetran API on creation, naming the schema and only the tables the warehouse reads, with exponential backoff on retry. Before it, a new tenant's whole schema synced, every table.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="when">Jan 2026</span>
        <span>The borrowing-base recovery: companies missing from the lender's loan tape traced to spreadsheet and backend data-integrity defects, fixed upstream after backend query approval, roughly half a million dollars of capacity back.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="when">Feb 2026</span>
        <span>Tenant-flattening fan-out pruned from 92 models to the 9 anything read. Pipeline queries fell from 13 million a month to under 2 million, and stayed there. Observability in the same month: Slack and email alerting on tapes, DAGs, and connectors, and a Streamlit dashboard over a dbt Cloud API ingestion pipeline landing in a vendor-data bronze database, mapping dependencies, refresh intervals, and schedule collisions.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="when">Apr 2026</span>
        <span>The retrieval architecture and corpus design for a customer-facing RAG assistant, written as the forcing function for a product nobody had scoped yet.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="when">Apr to Jun 2026</span>
        <span>Two vendor exits without an outage: dbt Cloud to Airflow on MWAA provisioned in Terraform, and four sources from Fivetran to self-hosted Airbyte with the VPC peerings adopted into state.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="when">Jun to Sep 2026</span>
        <span>Governance for the AI surfaces, the classifier evaluation platform, and per-call cost and adoption analytics.</span>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="decisions">
    <h2 id="decisions">Decisions I would defend</h2>
    <dl class="decisions">
      <div>
        <dt>Model to consumption, not to completeness</dt>
        <dd>
          The replication layer brought everything across in case someone
          needed it. Ninety-two models fanned a UNION across about 120 tenant
          schemas every night to serve nine tables anyone read. The fix was a
          config block disabling the rest, and the diff was almost entirely
          deletions. If I started again I would land nothing until something
          downstream asked for it.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Windows over recursion</dt>
        <dd>
          A recursive CTE for a running total or a forward-fill is a tell.
          Every recursion in the delinquency model became a window function
          over a shared date spine, and the result was measurable in query
          history: 617,770 rows in 12.7 seconds worst case, zero spill. The
          legacy view was phased out and deleted.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Every definition lives in exactly one place</dt>
        <dd>
          The paying-customer universe is versioned and enforced identically
          in the company dimension and the board segmentation mart, so the
          two cannot disagree. One macro defines the evaluation denominator
          for every ML metric, so offline and online scoring cannot drift
          apart. If two tables can disagree about a definition, eventually
          they will, and you find out in a board meeting.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Point-in-time, decided before it is expensive</dt>
        <dd>
          Any time a mutable attribute joins a historical fact, ask whether
          today's value is about to rewrite last quarter. Three SCD-2
          snapshots exist for that reason: grace periods, because the
          delinquency model rebuilds all history nightly; business status, so
          month-end counts use the status in effect; loyalty balances, because
          the source is current-state only.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Tenant isolation as a design input</dt>
        <dd>
          Every silver and gold table is keyed and clustered on the tenant so
          the row access policy prunes partitions instead of scanning. The
          policy checks role membership against an entitlements table, since
          a policy cannot trust session variables anyone can set. Masking is
          split by job: the eval reader sees free text and not dollar values.
          Because these tables are rebuilt with create-or-replace, each model
          re-attaches its own policy in a post-hook, and the proposal states
          in writing that the raw landing table is not covered.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Schedule on data, not on clocks</dt>
        <dd>
          Gold DAGs run when the silver DAG publishes an Airflow Dataset, not
          at a fixed hour, so gold rebuilds the moment silver lands and never
          on stale silver. Dataset URIs are declared once in a shared module,
          because Airflow matches on the string and a drifted literal fails
          silently.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Test the invariant, not the row count</dt>
        <dd>
          A not-null test says the pipeline ran. A monotonicity test says the
          business logic is still right: days past due go up by exactly one
          or reset to zero. Ending balance equals beginning plus spend minus
          payments, to the cent. Recoveries never exceed the write-off they
          net against. Approved company counts never decrease. Six of those
          sit beside 326 generic tests, and they are the half that matters.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Build order is a dependency</dt>
        <dd>
          The delinquency model was one day short and the SQL was fine. Its
          date spine filtered on a pre-computed "is past" flag from a
          dimension that rebuilt after it in the schedule, so yesterday was
          never past. The fix was a run-time cutoff. The lesson became a
          dependency-review checklist the repo now reviews every model change
          against: refresh cadence, build order, downstream blast radius, and
          never a pre-computed relative-date flag as a spine filter.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Write down what a metric does not measure</dt>
        <dd>
          One release criterion was no class recall drop over fifteen points.
          The eval mart's class-concentration columns look like they cover
          it and do not; they catch collapse to one class and miss one
          class's recall falling while the distribution holds. The limitation
          is in the model header, the columns are labeled a collapse canary,
          and the follow-up mart at the right grain is specified.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>The Terraform apply I did not run</dt>
        <dd>
          Adopting eight live VPC peerings into state, the plan also showed
          drift in another team's module: a different node type than the code
          declared, a public load balancer where the code said private.
          Applying would have replaced their node group and taken down
          ingestion. I merged the no-op, did not apply, wrote up the specific
          resources at risk, and took it to the team that owned them. When
          you are the only person in your function, the temptation is to
          just fix it. The right move was to make the risk legible.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="cost">
    <h2 id="cost">What it cost to run</h2>
    <p>
      Pipeline compute is the Snowflake credits burned rebuilding models on
      the warehouse the dbt service account uses, nothing else. Both series
      are from Snowflake's own account usage views, 2026.
    </p>
    <table class="cost">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Month</th><th scope="col">Pipeline credits</th><th scope="col">Pipeline queries</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {credits.map(([m, c, q]) => (
          <tr><th scope="row">{m}</th><td>{c.toLocaleString()}</td><td>{q}M</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The query collapse in March tracks the February pruning commit exactly.
      The credit drop in June tracks the vendor exits. A company restructuring
      in April reduced transaction volume in the same window, so part of the
      credit decline is organic. Pipeline compute is driven by model count and
      rebuild size far more than by customer volume, which is why the pruning
      and orchestration work is the dominant lever, but the fully attributable
      figures are the query series and the delinquency rebuild, measured on
      the same model and warehouse before and after.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="boundaries">
    <h2 id="boundaries">Boundaries</h2>
    <p>
      Things a reference check would turn up, so they are here first.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        I did not build the RAG serving service: the agent, the vector
        store, and its calibration harness belong to two colleagues. I
        authored the retrieval architecture and corpus design, built the
        classifier evaluation platform, the cost and adoption analytics, and
        the tenant governance model, and wrote the architecture recon of
        their service that the eval data contract was specced from.
      </li>
      <li>
        The AI analytics models shipped merged but disabled, pending the
        upstream event source. Built, reviewed, and lineage-validated; not
        yet producing rows when I left.
      </li>
      <li>
        The AWS side of the lender delivery pipeline predates me and has no
        infrastructure as code: the queues, notifications, and roles were
        console-built. I documented the whole path, including that.
      </li>
      <li>
        The platform-metrics domain had a colleague leading it. My share
        there is 19 of 64 commits. Every other domain on this page is mine
        by a wide margin, and the Airflow and governance repos are mine
        entirely.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede { font-size: 1.3rem; line-height: 1.4; margin-bottom: 1.75rem; }
  .diagram { overflow-x: auto; margin: 0 -1.25rem; padding: 0 1.25rem; }
  .diagram svg { display: block; min-width: 560px; width: 100%; height: auto; }
  .arc { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; }
  .arc li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: 0.1rem 1rem;
    padding: 0.8rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  @media (min-width: 600px) { .arc li { grid-template-columns: 8.5rem 1fr; } }
  .arc .when { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem; white-space: nowrap; }
  .decisions { margin: 0; }
  .decisions div { padding: 1.1rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .decisions dt { font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 0.3rem; }
  .decisions dd { margin: 0; }
  .cost { border-collapse: collapse; margin: 0 0 1.1rem; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .cost th, .cost td { text-align: right; padding: 0.3rem 1.25rem 0.3rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .cost th[scope='row'] { text-align: left; font-weight: 500; }
  .cost thead th { color: var(--muted); font-weight: 500; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  ul { padding-left: 1.2rem; }
  li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
</style>
